ne https://github.com/ai-hero-dev/cohort-002-project.git\n# Navigate into the repository\ncd cohort-002-project\n# Install dependencies\npnpm install\n```\n\n---\n\n## Two Types of Activities\n\nIn this cohort, we're going to be doing two types of activities: skill building or project work.\n\nEach day starts with skill building, where we do some problem and solution exercises and some explainers to build the skills we need. Then in the project work section, we go and apply those skills.\n\nThis should give a really nice balance of working on the skills individually and then applying them in a creative way.\n\n## Repository Structure\n\nThere are two different repos—each for skill building and for the project:\n\n| Repository     | Purpose                        | URL                                                                                               |\n| -------------- | ------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Skill Building | Problem and solution exercises | [AI Hero Dev Cohort 002 Skill Building](https://github.com/ai-hero-dev/cohort-002-skill-building) |\n| Project        | Creative application of skills | [AI Hero Dev Cohort 002 Project](https://github.com/ai-hero-dev/cohort-002-project)               |\n\n## Getting Started\n\nTo begin, you'll need to:\n\n1. Clone the [skill building repository](https://github.com/ai-hero-dev/cohort-002-skill-building) to your local setup\n2. Clone the [project repository](https://github.com/ai-hero-dev/cohort-002-project) separately\n3. Check the instructions in each repository if you need additional guidance\n\nOnce that's done, you're ready to move on to the next exercise.\n\nGood luck and see you in the next one!\n","free":true,"has_video":true,"id":"lesson-318mv","mux_playback_id":"rdz029fPDSo7WYMMK8GHbPvsVoD6zUQmTkX7YSwl01aqo","parentResources":[{"id":"workshop-pxqjv","slug":"before-we-start-pxqjv","state":"published","title":"Before We Start","type":"workshop","visibility":"public"}],"slug":"skill-building-vs-project-318mv","state":"published","summary":"Quick start guide for Cohort 002. Clone two repos—Skill Building (exercises) and Project (application)—to start AI engineering skills through practice.","thumbnail_time":33,"title":"Skill Building Vs. Project","type":"lesson","updated_at_timestamp":1764614456589,"video_resource_id":"explainer-RtTnRehd9.mp4","visibility":"unlisted","workshop_slug":"before-we-start-pxqjv"},"highlight":{},"highlights":[],"text_match":100,"text_match_info":{"best_field_score":"0","best_field_weight":12,"fields_matched":4,"num_tokens_dropped":1,"score":"100","tokens_matched":0,"typo_prefix_score":255}},"text_match":100,"text_match_info":{"best_field_score":"0","best_field_weight":12,"fields_matched":4,"num_tokens_dropped":1,"score":"100","tokens_matched":0,"typo_prefix_score":255}},{"container_title":"Build Your Own AI Personal Assistant in TypeScript","course_ids":["cohort-ytorn","workshop-pxqjv"],"created_at_timestamp":1764614400946,"description":"Welcome to this latest cohort, where we're going to be building a personal assistant in TypeScript. I wanted to build this course because we had such a good response to the previous cohort, but there was some stuff missing from there that I wanted to cover in depth.\n\n## Retrieval: The Foundation\n\nSpecifically, and this is the big one: retrieval. Being able to take an existing data set and plug it into an LLM, in other words, retrieve from that data set in response to questions, is one of the most powerful things you can do as an AI engineer.\n\nYou can build all sorts of cool things with retrieval:\n\n- Reading documentation\n- Taking existing data from a company and making it searchable and queryable\n- Building custom knowledge bases for specialized domains\n\nA lot of people ask me: should I train my own LLM in order to specialize it? No, probably not. You just need to get really good at retrieving from a private data source.\n\nWe've got two full days on retrieval in this cohort.\n\n## Evaluation and Production-Ready AI\n\nOn the third day, we're going to touch on evals. I know we're building personal software here, but really, every time you're building personal software, you should be training for production.\n\nBuilding evals is a key part of putting AI apps into production and making them better once they're there. We're going to learn how to evaluate retrieval systems as well as more complex tool calling systems too.\n\n## Memory and Learning\n\nOn day four, we're going to touch on something that I've never touched on before: memory. The whole idea of a personal assistant is that it learns from your preferences, from your behaviors, and from what you actually do. In theory, it should get better over time.\n\nSo in day four, we're going to build an assistant that actually learns from you, and we're going to look at all sorts of cool things you can do with memory.\n\n## Human in the Loop\n\nOn the fifth day, we're going to focus on Human in the Loop, which is an incredibly important pattern for allowing your AI to actually do things in the world.\n\nIf you've taken the [AI SDK v5 Crash Course](https://www.aihero.dev/workshops/ai-sdk-v5-crash-course), you know about tools, but how do you actually grant access to powerful tools to the LLM while keeping the user in full control?\n\nThe answer is Human in the Loop, and we're going to build it from scratch using the AI SDK's custom data parts API to the absolute maximum.\n\nOnce you finish that section, you'll know the custom data parts API back to front and you'll be able to build all sorts of powerful user interfaces with it.\n\n## Next Steps\n\nBest of luck in the cohort. I'll see you in the office hours, and I'll be around in the Discord if you have any more questions. Nice work and I'll see you in the next one.","free":true,"has_video":true,"id":"lesson-ojykw","mux_playback_id":"vCyb5kabKoEcXLB3erRk500IBbCESr00zclzz00ddzQTUY","parentResources":[{"id":"workshop-pxqjv","slug":"before-we-start-pxqjv","state":"published","title":"Before We Start","type":"workshop","visibility":"public"}],"slug":"course-overview-ojykw","state":"published","summary":"An AI engineering cohort focused on building a TypeScript personal assistant. Learn retrieval, evaluation, memory, and Human in the Loop patterns.","thumbnail_time":127,"title":"Course Overview","type":"lesson","updated_at_timestamp":1764614400946,"video_resource_id":"explainer-BxJ3X1mu.mp4","visibility":"unlisted","workshop_slug":"before-we-start-pxqjv","objectID":"lesson-ojykw","_snippetResult":{"container_title":{"value":"Build Your Own AI Personal Assistant in TypeScript","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"course_ids":[{"value":"cohort-ytorn","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},{"value":"workshop-pxqjv","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]}],"created_at_timestamp":{"value":"1764614400946","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"description":{"value":"Welcome to this latest cohort, where we're going to be building a personal assistant in TypeScript. I wanted to build this course because we had such a good response to the previous cohort, but there was some stuff missing from there that I wanted to cover in depth.\n\n## Retrieval: The Foundation\n\nSpecifically, and this is the big one: retrieval. Being able to take an existing data set and plug it into an LLM, in other words, retrieve from that data set in response to questions, is one of the most powerful things you can do as an AI engineer.\n\nYou can build all sorts of cool things with retrieval:\n\n- Reading documentation\n- Taking existing data from a company and making it searchable and queryable\n- Building custom knowledge bases for specialized domains\n\nA lot of people ask me: should I train my own LLM in order to specialize it? No, probably not. You just need to get really good at retrieving from a private data source.\n\nWe've got two full days on retrieval in this cohort.\n\n## Evaluation and Production-Ready AI\n\nOn the third day, we're going to touch on evals. I know we're building personal software here, but really, every time you're building personal software, you should be training for production.\n\nBuilding evals is a key part of putting AI apps into production and making them better once they're there. We're going to learn how to evaluate retrieval systems as well as more complex tool calling systems too.\n\n## Memory and Learning\n\nOn day four, we're going to touch on something that I've never touched on before: memory. The whole idea of a personal assistant is that it learns from your preferences, from your behaviors, and from what you actually do. In theory, it should get better over time.\n\nSo in day four, we're going to build an assistant that actually learns from you, and we're going to look at all sorts of cool things you can do with memory.\n\n## Human in the Loop\n\nOn the fifth day, we're going to focus on Human in the Loop, which is an incredibly important pattern for allowing your AI to actually do things in the world.\n\nIf you've taken the [AI SDK v5 Crash Course](https://www.aihero.dev/workshops/ai-sdk-v5-crash-course), you know about tools, but how do you actually grant access to powerful tools to the LLM while keeping the user in full control?\n\nThe answer is Human in the Loop, and we're going to build it from scratch using the AI SDK's custom data parts API to the absolute maximum.\n\nOnce you finish that section, you'll know the custom data parts API back to front and you'll be able to build all sorts of powerful user interfaces with it.\n\n## Next Steps\n\nBest of luck in the cohort. I'll see you in the office hours, and I'll be around in the Discord if you have any more questions. Nice work and I'll see you in the next one.","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"free":{"value":"true","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"has_video":{"value":"true","matchLevel":"none","match