amples of people jailbreaking the system prompt, but this is at least the theory.\n\n## Reasoning Tokens\n\nLots of models can send back reasoning tokens, where the model appears to think through its output before it responds. These reasoning tokens are just another part of the assistant message.\n\n![Reasoning Tokens](https://res.cloudinary.com/total-typescript/image/upload/v1759237358/ai-hero-images/pr2deu4owgqdygndbkrx.png)\n\nThis is an important concept here that messages can contain multiple parts. This is useful for file parts too, where you can send files to LLMs or get files back from them in image generation use cases.\n\n![Files](https://res.cloudinary.com/total-typescript/image/upload/v1759237359/ai-hero-images/wgx9n1uobwgugln1xg0d.png)\n\nFor instance here, we're sending a file to an LLM saying summarize this PDF, and it replies with a summary.\n\n## Tools\n\nWith tools you have the ability to say you have access to a write file tool to the LLM. This might write a file to our file system or something.\n\n![Tools](https://res.cloudinary.com/total-typescript/image/upload/v1759237360/ai-hero-images/at8gx2zr54m1ff1bbxyf.png)\n\nThen the user is going to say, write a `todo.md` file. The assistant then produces a tool call message. That tool call has an `id` on it. And it also has an instruction to write a file `todo.md` with empty contents.\n\nNow we, in our applications, take that tool call and execute it, and we send back to the LLM a tool result with the same `id` as the tool call and the result of what happened, usually as a string.\n\nWe get back from the assistant a summary of what happened, \"wrote a todo.md file successfully\".\n\nSo tools are kind of like a conversation between the LLM and our system, where:\n\n1. The tool call is the LLM asking us for something\n2. The tool result is us giving it the information it needs\n\nThat's how AI-powered apps like Claude Code and Cursor can do things on your system. We'll dig deeper into tools later.\n","free":true,"has_video":true,"id":"post_qnxn9","image":"https://image.mux.com/ijjkttEVlQOxo52Nw9RnbALKYThlfkPNvhhzLseJOi4/thumbnail.jpg?width=720\u0026height=405\u0026fit_mode=smartcrop\u0026time=100.836556","mux_playback_id":"ijjkttEVlQOxo52Nw9RnbALKYThlfkPNvhhzLseJOi4","popularity_30d":5312,"slug":"messages-system-prompts-and-reasoning-tokens","state":"published","summary":"Learn how conversations with AI work under the hood. Explore user messages, system prompts, reasoning tokens, and tool calls.","thumbnail_time":101,"title":"Messages, System Prompts and Reasoning Tokens","type":"post","updated_at_timestamp":1759238765479,"video_resource_id":"explainer-jlB3gRskY.mp4","visibility":"public"},"highlight":{},"highlights":[],"text_match":100,"text_match_info":{"best_field_score":"0","best_field_weight":12,"fields_matched":4,"num_tokens_dropped":1,"score":"100","tokens_matched":0,"typo_prefix_score":255}},"text_match":100,"text_match_info":{"best_field_score":"0","best_field_weight":12,"fields_matched":4,"num_tokens_dropped":1,"score":"100","tokens_matched":0,"typo_prefix_score":255}},{"course_ids":["list_zoubx"],"created_at_timestamp":1759225624967,"description":"Tokens are the fundamental building blocks that help Large Language Models (LLMs) process text. Understanding them is essential, especially since you're billed based on token usage.\n\n## What Are Tokens?\n\nTokens are simply numbers that represent how the LLM \"thinks\" about the text you provide. The process of converting text into tokens is called **encoding**.\n\nThe tokenization process works in two parts:\n\n1. The tokenizer splits text into tokens it recognizes\n2. These tokens are converted into numbers\n\n![Encoding](https://res.cloudinary.com/total-typescript/image/upload/v1759238445/ai-hero-images/vqsgrg2rv79rqgxhvsmc.png)\n\nDecoding is the reverse process:\n\n1. Numbers are converted back into text tokens\n2. The tokens are joined together to form the output\n\n![Decoding](https://res.cloudinary.com/total-typescript/image/upload/v1759238446/ai-hero-images/t4k9fszkfkcbm1mrthwj.png)\n\n## The LLM Process Flow\n\nThe complete LLM process looks like this:\n\n1. Tokenizer encodes your input text into tokens\n2. LLM processes your tokens\n3. LLM produces output tokens\n4. Output tokens are decoded back into readable text\n\n![LLM Process Flow](https://res.cloudinary.com/total-typescript/image/upload/v1759238447/ai-hero-images/hs0gx3ozagimhl5xunxn.png)\n\nTo clarify, input tokens include:\n\n- Your conversation history with the LLM\n- System prompts\n- Tool definitions\n\nOutput tokens are what the LLM sends back as a response.\n\nYou're billed for both input and output tokens, typically at different rates. One way to save money is to design your prompts to generate fewer output tokens.\n\n## How Tokens Are Created\n\nThe tokenization process starts with a large corpus of text - similar to what's used to train the LLM itself. Let's imagine a tiny corpus consisting of just one sentence: \"the cat sat on the mat.\"\n\n![Tokenization](https://res.cloudinary.com/total-typescript/image/upload/v1759238448/ai-hero-images/ekv9gq9bvk9efycqow1p.png)\n\nFirst, all individual characters are extracted:\n\n```\nT H E space C A T space S A T space O N space T H E space M A T\n```\n\nEach of these characters becomes its own token in the vocabulary.\n\nNext, common groupings of characters are identified:\n\n- \"TH\" appears in \"the\" (twice)\n- \"HE\" appears in \"the\" (twice)\n- \"AT\" appears in \"cat\", \"sat\", and \"mat\"\n\nEach of these groupings also gets assigned its own token.\n\nThen, groups of groups are identified - like \"TH\" + \"HE\" creating \"THE\" (the word \"the\"), which gets its own token.\n\n## Vocabulary Size Matters\n\nThe goal is to create a large vocabulary of tokens because larger vocabularies can split words into fewer tokens, making processing more efficient.\n\n![Vocabulary Size](https://res.cloudinary.com/total-typescript/image/upload/v1759238449/ai-hero-images/mcsd9qzjf4cz1ulsrblz.png)\n\nFor example, a vocabulary size of 1,000 tokens might split \"understanding\" into 5 tokens. A vocabulary size of 50,000 tokens might split it into 3 tokens, and a vocabulary size of 200,000 tokens might split it into 2 tokens.\n\nHaving a larger vocabulary means you can split words into fewer tokens, making processing more efficient.\n\n## Handling Unusual Words\n\nThe tokenizer struggles with uncommon words. For example, \"O Frabjous Day\" from Lewis Carroll's poem gets split into many tokens because \"Frabjous\" is a made-up word that doesn't appear frequently in the training corpus.\n\n![Unusual Words](https://res.cloudinary.com/total-typescript/image/upload/v1759238450/ai-hero-images/pcnihurikeetpfh01xss.png)\n\nWe can see that it turns it into 7 tokens - more than we'd expect from only 15 characters.\n\n## Final Thoughts\n\nI hope that helps demystify tokens a bit. I found the [tiktokenizer playground](https://tiktokenizer.vercel.app/) really useful for understanding this stuff.\n\nLet me know if you have any questions - and what else would you like me to cover next?\n\nMatt\n","free":true,"has_video":true,"id":"post_36lhg","image":"https://image.mux.com/ogTZkRIjv00qmTh6B8MpqpSdKOVl2lYQHUcZOohLg007o/thumbnail.jpg?width=720\u0026height=405\u0026fit_mode=smartcrop\u0026time=0","mux_playback_id":"ogTZkRIjv00qmTh6B8MpqpSdKOVl2lYQHUcZOohLg007o","popularity_30d":0,"slug":"what-are-tokens","state":"published","summary":"Discover how tokens power LLMs, affecting costs and efficiency. Learn about encoding, decoding, and vocabulary size for optimized text processing.","thumbnail_time":0,"title":"What Are Tokens?","type":"post","updated_at_timestamp":1759225624967,"video_resource_id":"01fundamentals0102tokensexplainer-ioWZHSZGM.mp4","visibility":"public","objectID":"post_36lhg","_snippetResult":{"course_ids":[{"value":"list_zoubx","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]}],"created_at_timestamp":{"value":"1759225624967","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"description":{"value":"Tokens are the fundamental building blocks that help Large Language Models (LLMs) process text. Understanding them is essential, especially since you're billed based on token usage.\n\n## What Are Tokens?\n\nTokens are simply numbers that represent how the LLM \"thinks\" about the text you provide. The process of converting text into tokens is called **encoding**.\n\nThe tokenization process works in two parts:\n\n1. The tokenizer splits text into tokens it recognizes\n2. These tokens are converted into numbers\n\n![Encoding](https://res.cloudinary.com/total-typescript/image/upload/v1759238445/ai-hero-images/vqsgrg2rv79rqgxhvsmc.png)\n\nDecoding is the reverse process:\n\n1. Numbers are converted back into text tokens\n2. The tokens are joined together to form the output\n\n![Decoding](https://res.cloudinary.com/total-typescript/image/upload/v1759238446/ai-hero-images/t4k9fszkfkcbm1mrthwj.png)\n\n## The LLM Process Flow\n\nThe complete LLM process looks like this:\n\n1. Tokenizer encodes your input text into tokens\n2. LLM processes your tokens\n3. LLM produces output tokens\n4. Output tokens are decoded back into readable text\n\n![LLM Process Flow](https://res.cloudinary.com/total-typescript/image/upload/v1759238447/ai-hero-images/hs0gx3ozagimhl5xunxn.png)\n\nTo clarify, input tokens include:\n\n- Your conversation history with the LLM\n- System prompts\n- Tool definitions\n\nOutput tokens are what the LLM sends back as a response.\n\nYou're billed for both input and output tokens, typically at different rates. One way to save money is to design your prompts to generate fewer output tokens.\n\n## How Tokens Are Created\n\nThe tokenization process starts with a large corpus of text - similar to what's used to train the LLM itself. Let's imagine a tiny corpus consisting of just one sentence: \"the cat sat on the mat.\"\n\n![Tokenization](https://res.cloudinary.com/total-typescript/image/upload/v1759238448/ai-hero-images/ekv9gq9bvk9efycqow1p.png)\n\nFirst, all individual characters are extracted:\n\n```\nT H E space C A T space S A T space O N space T H E space M A T\n```\n\nEach of these characters becomes its own token in the vocabulary.\n\nNext, common groupings of characters are identified:\n\n- \"TH\" appears in \"the\" (twice)\n- \"HE\" appears in \"the\" (twice)\n- \"AT\" appears in \"cat\", \"sat\", and \"mat\"\n\nEach of these groupings also gets assigned its own token.\n\nThen, groups of groups are identified - like \"TH\" + \"HE\" creating \"THE\" (the word \"the\"), which gets its own token.\n\n## Vocabulary Size Matters\n\nThe goal is to create a large vocabulary of tokens because larger vocabularies can split words into fewer tokens, making processing more efficient.\n\n![Vocabulary Size](https://res.cloudinary.com/total-typescript/image/upload/v1759238449/ai-hero-images/mcsd9qzjf4cz1ulsrblz.png)\n\nFor example, a vocabulary size of 1,000 tokens might split \"understanding\" into 5 tokens. A vocabulary size of 50,000 tokens might split it into 3 tokens, and a vocabulary size of 200,000 tokens might split it into 2 tokens.\n\nHaving a larger vocabulary means you can split words into fewer tokens, making processing more efficient.\n\n## Handling Unusual Words\n\nThe tokenizer struggles with uncommon words. For example, \"O Frabjous Day\" from Lewis Carroll's poem gets split into many tokens because \"Frabjous\" is a made-up word that doesn't appear frequently in the training corpus.\n\n![Unusual Words](https://res.cloudinary.com/total-typescript/image/upload/v1759238450/ai-hero-images/pcnihurikeetpfh01xss.png)\n\nWe can see that it turns it into 7 tokens - more than we'd expect from only 15 characters.\n\n## Final Thoughts\n\nI hope that helps demystify tokens a bit. I found the [tiktokenizer playground](https://tiktokenizer.vercel.app/) really useful for understanding this stuff.\n\nLet me know if you have any questions - and what else would you like me to cover next?\n\nMatt\n","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"free":{"value":"true","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"has_video":{"value":"true","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"id":{"value":"post_36lhg","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"image":{"value":"https://image.mux.com/ogTZkRIjv00qmTh6B8MpqpSdKOVl2lYQHUcZOohLg007o/thumbnail.jpg?width=720\u0026height=405\u0026fit_mode=smartcrop\u0026time=0","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"mux_playback_id":{"value":"ogTZkRIjv00qmTh6B8MpqpSdKOVl2lYQHUcZOohLg007o","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"popularity_30d":{"value":"0","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"slug":{"value":"what-are-tokens","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"state":{"value":"published","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"summary":{"value":"Discover how tokens power LLMs, affecting costs and efficiency. Learn about encoding, decoding, and vocabulary size for optimized text processing.","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"thumbnail_time":{"value":"0","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"title":{"value":"What Are Tokens?","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"type":{"value":"post","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"updated_at_timestamp":{"value":"1759225624967","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"video_resource_id":{"value":"01fundamentals0102tokensexplainer-ioWZHSZGM.mp4","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"visibility":{"value":"public","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]}},"_highlightResult":{"course_ids":[{"value":"list_zoubx","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]}],"created_at_timestamp":{"value":"1759225624967","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"description":{"value":"Tokens are the fundamental building blocks that help Large Language Models (LLMs) process text. Understanding them is essential, especially since you're billed based on token usage.\n\n## What Are Tokens?\n\nTokens are simply numbers that represent how the LLM \"thinks\" about the text you provide. The process of converting text into tokens is called **encoding**.\n\nThe tokenization process works in two parts:\n\n1. The tokenizer splits text into tokens it recognizes\n2. These tokens are converted into numbers\n\n![Encoding](https://res.cloudinary.com/total-typescript/image/upload/v1759238445/ai-hero-images/vqsgrg2rv79rqgxhvsmc.png)\n\nDecoding is the reverse process:\n\n1. Numbers are converted back into text tokens\n2. The tokens are joined together to form the output\n\n![Decoding](https://res.cloudinary.com/total-typescript/image/upload/v1759238446/ai-hero-images/t4k9fszkfkcbm1mrthwj.png)\n\n## The LLM Process Flow\n\nThe complete LLM process looks like this:\n\n1. Tokenizer encodes your input text into tokens\n2. LLM processes your tokens\n3. LLM produces output tokens\n4. Output tokens are decoded back into readable text\n\n![LLM Process Flow](https://res.cloudinary.com/total-typescript/image/upload/v1759238447/ai-hero-images/hs0gx3ozagimhl5xunxn.png)\n\nTo clarify, input tokens include:\n\n- Your conversation history with the LLM\n- System prompts\n- Tool definitions\n\nOutput tokens are what the LLM sends back as a response.\n\nYou're billed for both input and output tokens, typically at different rates. One way to save money is to design your prompts to generate fewer output tokens.\n\n## How Tokens Are Created\n\nThe tokenization process starts with a large corpus of text - similar to what's used to train the LLM itself. Let's imagine a tiny corpus consisting of just one sentence: \"the cat sat on the mat.\"\n\n![Tokenization](https://res.cloudinary.com/total-typescript/image/upload/v1759238448/ai-hero-images/ekv9gq9bvk9efycqow1p.png)\n\nFirst, all individual characters are extracted:\n\n```\nT H E space C A T space S A T space O N space T H E space M A T\n```\n\nEach of these characters becomes its own token in the vocabulary.\n\nNext, common groupings of characters are identified:\n\n- \"TH\" appears in \"the\" (twice)\n- \"HE\" appears in \"the\" (twice)\n- \"AT\" appears in \"cat\", \"sat\", and \"mat\"\n\nEach of these groupings also gets assigned its own token.\n\nThen, groups of groups are identified - like \"TH\" + \"HE\" creating \"THE\" (the word \"the\"), which gets its own token.\n\n## Vocabulary Size Matters\n\nThe goal is to create a large vocabulary of tokens because larger vocabularies can split words into fewer tokens, making processing more efficient.\n\n![Vocabulary Size](https://res.cloudinary.com/total-typescript/image/upload/v1759238449/ai-hero-images/mcsd9qzjf4cz1ulsrblz.png)\n\nFor example, a vocabulary size of 1,000 tokens might split \"understanding\" into 5 tokens. A vocabulary size of 50,000 tokens might split it into 3 tokens, and a vocabulary size of 200,000 tokens might split it into 2 tokens.\n\nHaving a larger vocabulary means you can split words into fewer tokens, making processing more efficient.\n\n## Handling Unusual Words\n\nThe tokenizer struggles with uncommon words. For example, \"O Frabjous Day\" from Lewis Carroll's poem gets split into many tokens because \"Frabjous\" is a made-up word that doesn't appear frequently in the training corpus.\n\n![Unusual Words](https://res.cloudinary.com/total-typescript/image/upload/v1759238450/ai-hero-images/pcnihurikeetpfh01xss.png)\n\nWe can see that it turns it into 7 tokens - more than we'd expect from only 15 characters.\n\n## Final Thoughts\n\nI hope that helps demystify tokens a bit. I found the [tiktokenizer playground](https://tiktokenizer.vercel.app/) really useful for understanding this stuff.\n\nLet me know if you have any questions - and what else would you like me to cover next?\n\nMatt\n","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"free":{"value":"true","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"has_video":{"value":"true","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"id":{"value":"post_36lhg","matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[]},"image":{"value":"https://image.mux.com/ogTZkRIjv00qmTh6B8MpqpSdKOVl2lYQHUcZOohLg007o/thumbnail.jpg?width=720\u0026height=405\u0026f